Campaign clip-wire-push, run 2026-08-06 against a150df8 in an isolated
worktree. Result:

    SURVIVED  IDENTITY CONTROL                          (correct)
    killed    crop preservation removed
    killed    domain taken from the SOURCE model
    SURVIVED  no-extent guard removed                   <-- see below
    killed    null volume accepted
    killed    the push never writes

    killed 4  SURVIVED 2  INVALID 0  UNSCORED 0

Baseline was green before scoring and the identity control survived, so these
verdicts are readable rather than noise.

ARM 4 IS AN EQUIVALENT MUTANT. DO NOT "CLOSE" IT.

The arm deletes the extent guard at the top of PushClipToVolume:

    if (PhysicalSize.X <= 0.0 || PhysicalSize.Y <= 0.0 || PhysicalSize.Z <= 0.0)
    {
        return false;
    }

It survives, and the first reading of that -- the reading this file exists to
head off -- is "the branch is untested, go write a test". That reading is
wrong, and it is wrong in the direction that damages the code.

The behaviour IS tested. FlowVizWorkspaceVolumeSeamTest.cpp builds a bare
UCFDVizVolumeComponent, asserts it really has no domain, pushes into it, and
asserts both that the push is refused AND that the volume was left unclipped
rather than half-applied.

The arm survives because the guard is REDUNDANT, which the guard's own comment
already says. With it deleted, control reaches

    if (!Pushed.SetDomainSize(PhysicalSize).IsOk()) { return false; }

whose predicate is character-for-character identical to the deleted one
(FlowVizClipViewModel.cpp). Nothing between the two lines touches the volume --
the only statements are const reads of Source -- and Volume->SetClip is reached
only after both. Same return value, same absence of a write, on every input.
There is no observable difference to detect, so no test can kill this arm.

The hazard is what "closing the gap" would require. To make the arm killable
you would have to weaken SetDomainSize's check so the two stop coinciding --
loosening a real guard on a real view model in order to score a mutation arm.
That is a worse codebase in exchange for a better-looking number.

The guard stays. It refuses on the VOLUME's terms ("no case is bound") while
SetDomainSize refuses on the clip model's terms ("that domain is not usable"),
and they coincide only because GetPhysicalSize happens to return exactly
ZeroVector today. Keeping it also puts the refusal where a reader looking for
"what does this function refuse" will actually find it.

Repo memory unreachable-code-cannot-be-differentially-tested covers this shape:
when an earlier guard blocks the failing input, test the primitive directly
instead. That is already done -- SetDomainSize's refusal of zero, negative and
NaN axes is asserted directly in FlowVizClipViewModelTest.cpp.

Arm 1 is the identity control and must SURVIVE. If it ever does not, the
harness is scoring noise and every line above is void.

The rationale lives here rather than at the top of the .txt because
parse_mutants.py splits on `%%` and raises ValueError on any record that is not
exactly three `--`-separated fields. A prose header would abort the campaign.
